(* X25519 cofactor reduction — machine-checked projection identity.

   Code: libVELA/vela-crypto/src/kem.rs
     - decapsulate(): x25519_ss = sk.x25519_sk.diffie_hellman(&epk)
       (line 159), reject when !was_contributory() (lines 160-162).
     - encapsulate(): the same check on the recipient key (lines 136-141).
   Crate: x25519-dalek 2.0.1 / curve25519-dalek 4.1.3.  diffie_hellman uses
   MontgomeryPoint::mul_clamped, i.e. the scalar is clamped to a multiple of
   8 (curve25519-dalek scalar.rs:1386-1391), and was_contributory() tests
   that the 32-byte shared secret is not all zero (x25519.rs:324-326).

   CryptoVerif 2.12's shipped DH_X25519 macro models Curve25519 with the
   cofactor k = 8:
     - G is the full group (curve and twist elements),
     - Z is the set of private keys, which are multiples of 8 and prime to
       the two prime orders,
     - subG = {X^8} is the prime-order subgroup, g_k = g^8, pow_k(Y) = Y^8,
       exp_div_k(X,y) = X^(y/8), and subGtoG is the injection subG -> G,
     - is_zero_G / is_zero_subG are the zero-u-coordinate predicates.
   See default.ocvl lines 2765-2889 and 3385-3394, justified in
   https://hal.inria.fr/hal-02100345 (WireGuard proof).

   This file proves, for every full-group element Y and every exponent x in
   the clamped set Z, that
     exp(Y, x) = subGtoG(exp_div_k(pow_k(Y), x))          (shared secret)
     is_zero_G(exp(Y, x)) = is_zero_G(subGtoG(pow_k(Y)))  (reject test).
   Both are proved by the DH_subgroup equations with probability 0 (exact),
   not by an assumption:
     - exp(Y,x) = subGtoG(exp_div_k(pow_k(Y),x)) is the DH_subgroup
       equation in default.ocvl (line 2851);
     - is_zero_G(subGtoG(e)) = is_zero_subG(e) and
       is_zero_subG(exp_div_k(e,x)) = is_zero_subG(e) are the
       DH_subgroup_with_is_neutral equations (lines 2883-2889), so the
       zero test depends only on pow_k(Y).

   Consequently the small-order component of Y is irrelevant: only the
   prime-order projection pow_k(Y) enters the shared secret and the reject
   check.  The companion kem_v2_x25519_cofactor_sha256.ocv uses that
   projection as its GDH group and models the small-order component as an
   explicit T component.

   This is the reduction step only.  It does not prove that Rust clamps
   keys or encodes points the way DH_X25519 assumes; the map from wire
   bytes to the macro's elements remains an argued correspondence.

   Expected: "All queries proved."
 *)

param N.

type Z [large,bounded,nonuniform].
type G [large,bounded].
type subG [large,bounded].
expand DH_X25519(G, Z, g, exp, mult, subG, g_k, exp_div_k, exp_div_k', pow_k, subGtoG, is_zero_G, is_zero_subG).

(* Y <-R G covers arbitrary full-group inputs, including low-order points;
   x <-R Z covers the clamped (multiple-of-8) exponents.  The equivalence is
   proved for N independent sessions, so it is not a single-query lemma. *)
equivalence
  foreach i <= N do O() := Y <-R G; x <-R Z;
          return(exp(Y, x), is_zero_G(exp(Y, x)))

  foreach i <= N do O() := Y <-R G; x <-R Z;
          return(subGtoG(exp_div_k(pow_k(Y), x)),
                 is_zero_G(subGtoG(pow_k(Y))))
